00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:04.230 align:start position:0%
there is some cause for tentative  optimism in these jobs figures but there  are also challenges ahead as we move  through into the autumn so first this is  the number of people in payroll jobs you  can see that big dip there as the  pandemic hit and those figures gradually  recovering this year the estimates for  august are that 29.1  million people are now in paye jobs  that's back to pre-pandemic levels now  that's obviously good news but we should  be cautious about this the unemployment  rate is still higher than it was and we  don't yet know how many more people will  be looking for work when that furlough  scheme ends  but this is what will be really worrying  some businesses despite more people  being back in jobs the number of  vacancies is now the highest it's been  at any point since records began and  while this might be not a bad thing for  workers we know it's causing supply  chain delays and it's increasingly  difficult for small business owners  amikay is one of them he runs a  restaurant and he's struggling to find  the extra four staff he needs  we're looking for positions as uh you  know waitresses and restaurant  supervisors  um kitchen assistants um sous chefs  so it's kind of right through the the  the hierarchy levels you just don't have  the number of team members that we need  um to in the hours and days that we  would like to  now vacancies are being largely driven  by certain key sectors as you can see  many thousands of people are needed in  sectors like social and health in the  wholesale and retail sector and in  others as well but the real question is  whether all of these available jobs can  or will be filled by people who are  still unemployed or unfurloughed and  that is by no means guaranteed now these  new bars in color over the top there  show the number of furloughed people in  each of those sectors and as you can see  in parts of the economy there are  actually more people who need jobs than  there are jobs available that implies  the jobs are perhaps not in the right  place or at the right level for the  workers and it may well be harder to  find a new job depending on where you  live as you can see the unemployment  rate varies significantly across the  country as high as six percent in london  and as low as 3.3 percent in the  southwest given that london also has the  highest number of people on furlough  there could be significant competition  for jobs there what's clear is that the  pandemic has changed the economy forever  as it realigns both employers and  employees will have to adapt
 

